// WAF Logging Stopped (T1685.002)
//
// The technique disables the telemetry the detection would use, so this query runs against
// CLOUDTRAIL — the control plane — not against the WAF traffic log. Every call below
// survives the WAF stream stopping, which is the whole point.
//
// Dialect: Sentinel / Azure Log Analytics KQL over AWSCloudTrail. Flattened columns.
AWSCloudTrail
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where EventSource == "wafv2.amazonaws.com"
| where EventName in ("PutLoggingConfiguration", "DeleteLoggingConfiguration")
| where isempty(ErrorCode)
| extend Req = parse_json(RequestParameters)
| extend WebAclArn = tostring(coalesce(Req.resourceArn, Req.ResourceArn,
                                       Req.loggingConfiguration.resourceArn))
// A LoggingFilter with DefaultBehavior DROP and no keeping filter silences the stream while
// the configuration remains present and the console still shows logging enabled. This is
// the quiet variant an absence alert cannot distinguish from a delete.
| extend Filter        = Req.loggingConfiguration.loggingFilter
| extend DefaultDrop   = tostring(Filter.DefaultBehavior) =~ "DROP"
| extend FilterPresent = isnotnull(Filter)
| extend KeepFilters   = array_length(todynamic(Filter.Filters))
| extend Redacted      = array_length(todynamic(Req.loggingConfiguration.redactedFields))
// ARN parsing: for an assumed-role ARN the LAST segment is the session name and the SECOND
// is the role name. One idiom does not serve both forms.
| extend ArnParts  = split(UserIdentityArn, "/")
| extend Actor     = iff(UserIdentityArn contains ":assumed-role/",
                         tostring(ArnParts[1]),
                         tostring(ArnParts[array_length(ArnParts) - 1]))
| summarize
    Calls      = count(),
    Deletes    = countif(EventName == "DeleteLoggingConfiguration"),
    Replaces   = countif(EventName == "PutLoggingConfiguration"),
    AnyDrop    = max(DefaultDrop),
    AnyRedact  = max(coalesce(Redacted, 0) > 0),
    MinKeeps   = min(coalesce(KeepFilters, 0)),
    SourceIPs  = make_set(SourceIpAddress, 5),
    FirstSeen  = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastSeen   = max(TimeGenerated)
    by WebAclArn, Actor, UserIdentityArn
| extend Verdict = case(
    Deletes > 0,
        "WAF LOGGING DELETED — the web ACL still evaluates and blocks, and writes nothing. The gap is unrecoverable, P0",
    AnyDrop and MinKeeps == 0,
        "LOGGING FILTER SET TO DROP WITH NO KEEPING FILTER — configuration present, console shows enabled, stream silent, P0",
    AnyDrop,
        "LOGGING FILTER INSTALLED WITH DefaultBehavior DROP — read the Filters array; anything not explicitly kept is discarded, P1",
    AnyRedact,
        "REDACTED FIELDS ADDED — those components now log as the literal string xxx. Confirm intent, P2",
    "WAF logging configuration replaced — read the resulting configuration before dispositioning, P2")
| project FirstSeen, LastSeen, WebAclArn, Verdict, Actor, UserIdentityArn,
          Deletes, Replaces, AnyDrop, MinKeeps, AnyRedact, SourceIPs
| order by LastSeen desc
//
// ============================================================================
// WHY AN ABSENCE ALERT IS NOT ENOUGH, AND WHAT IT CANNOT TELL YOU
// ============================================================================
// The source rule alerts when no WAF records arrive in a window. Four things produce that,
// and only one is an incident:
//   1. DeleteLoggingConfiguration        — the incident.
//   2. A LoggingFilter of DefaultBehavior DROP — the incident, and invisible to
//      GetLoggingConfiguration reading as "enabled".
//   3. The delivery permission removed (log-group resource policy, S3 bucket policy,
//      Firehose service-linked role) — the incident, and WAF's own config is untouched.
//   4. GENUINELY NO TRAFFIC — a dev endpoint at night, a decommissioned ACL, a DNS cutover.
// An absence alert reports all four identically. That is why the deployable rules watch the
// control plane instead, and why the absence alert is kept only as a corroborating signal.
//
// ============================================================================
// WHAT IS UNRECOVERABLE
// ============================================================================
// For any window in which the web ACL had no logging configuration there is no per-request
// record and there never will be. Nothing reconstructs it. Three weaker things survive:
//   * CloudWatch AWS/WAFV2 metrics — counts only, no request content, 1-minute resolution,
//     governed by VisibilityConfig.CloudWatchMetricsEnabled (independent of logging).
//   * GetSampledRequests — a random sample from the first 5,000 requests in a chosen window
//     within the PREVIOUS THREE HOURS ONLY, MaxItems 1-500. It has NO query string and NO
//     body, so it is not a substitute even inside its window.
//   * Amazon Security Lake, if separately configured.
// The three-hour sampling window is the only one that expires, which is why evidence
// capture precedes remediation in this playbook rather than following it.
